{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the people saw that Moses delayed.</b> I have seen, I have truly seen, that most of the commentators who draw near to explain every obscure matter in the Torah sought to find fitting words to remove from the holy seed of Abraham the disgrace of evil speech and the calumny that went forth against them on account of this condemnable deed, for 'while the king sat at his table, my spikenard gave forth its fragrance' (Song of Songs 1:12). And most of the commentators agreed to hang the collar on the neck of the mixed multitude, the teachers of evil, and they were the adversaries of Israel and sinned first, and after them Israel also went astray. Therefore, for the honor of the whole house of Israel, I saw fit to write, in concise form, the principal meaning drawn from the words of most of the commentators, and included there as well are things that I added of my own to settle the linguistic fine points, and I will not set my mind on the difficulties and doubts that arise in the matter of this narrative, for my intention is only to explain the content of the matter to proclaim the purity of Israel. And this is its explanation:" "<b>And the people saw.</b> This is the mixed multitude, for everywhere the expression 'the people' is used of the lowly ones, as Rashi explained in the portion of בְּהַעֲלֹתְךָ on the verse 'and the people were as complainers' (Numbers 11:1). 'That Moses delayed' — because it was known to them that they did not go up from Egypt by the word of HaShem but rather Moses on his own brought them up and did not ask the mouth of HaShem, as it is written 'Go down, for your people has corrupted itself' (Exodus 32:7) — and not 'my people.' And correspondingly Moses said: 'If it is so, why should HaShem be angry against Your people' (Exodus 32:11) — and not 'against my people.' And the mixed multitude feared that if Moses did not come there, HaShem would command to expel them from the midst of the congregation, lest they cause Israel to sin with the idols of Egypt, for 'converts are as troublesome to Israel as a sore' (Yevamot 47b). And the fact that He had not expelled them until now, they thought was for Moses' honor. Therefore they devised a scheme to seek another leader in place of Moses, as it says 'this Moses, the man — we do not know what has become of him' (Exodus 32:1). That is to say: we do not know wherein his power is great, for certainly he possessed the image of some star through whose power he led and performed miracles, and we do not know what to do with the images, for we did not know what he had from the depiction. And you, his brother, know without doubt what that instrument was; therefore we want you to make us some likeness that will be an intermediary between us and the stars. And the Satan deceived them, for he showed them the likeness of Moses' bier between heaven and earth, to say that he had been like an intermediary between heaven and earth. Therefore they wanted some other intermediary. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 63a) that they desired many gods, because they did not know which form to choose."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:42.004250246Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}